<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulayman al-Baruni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Suleiman al-Baruni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulayman al-Baruni was an Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> scholar and politician from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains">Nafusa Mountains</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. A member of the Ottoman parliament, he led resistance to the Italian invasion from 1911 and in 1918 was one of the leaders of the Tripolitanian Republic. He later lived in exile and served as an adviser in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulayman-al-baruni.jpg" alt="Sulayman al-Baruni" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AC%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%AF_%D8%B3%D9%84%D9%8A%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%88%D9%86%D9%8A.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
